issue_context.body:
The following DualMode `ConnectAsync` code works as expected:

```C#
using Socket server = MakeServer(out int port);
string hostname = "[::FFFF:127.0.0.1]";
DnsEndPoint ep = new DnsEndPoint(hostname, port);
using Socket socket0 = new Socket(SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
await socket0.ConnectAsync(ep);

static Socket MakeServer(out int port) {
    Socket server = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
    server.Bind(new IPEndPoint(IPAddress.Loopback, 0));
    port = ((IPEndPoint)server.LocalEndPoint).Port;
    server.Listen();
    _ = server.AcceptAsync();
    return server;
}
```

while "equivalent" `Socket.ConnectAsync(AddressFamily,SocketType,SocketAsyncEventArgs)` connect code fails on both Windows and Linux:

```C#
using Socket server = MakeServer(out int port);
string hostname = "[::FFFF:127.0.0.1]";
DnsEndPoint ep = new DnsEndPoint(hostname, port);

SocketAsyncEventArgs a = new SocketAsyncEventArgs { RemoteEndPoint = ep };

ManualResetEvent mre = new ManualResetEvent(false);
a.Completed += (_, __) =>
{
    mre.Set();
};

if (Socket.ConnectAsync(SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp, a))
{
    mre.WaitOne();
}

if (a.SocketError != SocketError.Success)
{
    throw new SocketException((int)a.SocketError);
}
```

The reason is that the internal implementation [is creating a non-dual-mode socket](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/57bfe474518ab5b7cfe6bf7424a79ce3af9d6657/src/libraries/System.Net.Sockets/src/System/Net/Sockets/SocketAsyncEventArgs.cs#L712) for which the underlying native connect calls fail to connect to an IPV6-mapped IPv4 endpoint. (`10049 - WSAEADDRNOTAVAIL` on Windows).

We can easily fix this by detecting `address.IsIPv4MappedToIPv6` and either creating an IPv4 socket, or enabling `DualMode` in such cases.

